<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Two places a given distance apart</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(P\) and \(Q\) are both on the meridian \(37^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\). \(P\) is north of the equator and \(Q\) is south of it. The distance \(PQ\) along the meridian is \({@D1@}\) nm. Give a possible latitude for \(P\) and for \(Q\).</p><p>\(P\): [[input:ans1]] \(^\circ\,\mathrm{N}\), \(Q\): [[input:ans2]] \(^\circ\,\mathrm{S}\) [[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(b) \(R\) and \(S\) are both on the meridian \(37^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\) and both north of the equator, \({@D2@}\) nm apart. Give possible latitudes.</p><p>\(R\): [[input:ans3]] \(^\circ\,\mathrm{N}\), \(S\): [[input:ans4]] \(^\circ\,\mathrm{N}\) [[validation:ans3]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p><p>There are many correct answers. Decimals are allowed.</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Along a meridian, \(1\) nm is \(1'\) of latitude, so \({@D1@}\) nm is \({@D1@}' = {@k1@}^\circ\). \(P\) and \(Q\) are on opposite sides of the equator, so their latitudes add to \({@k1@}^\circ\): for example \(P\) at \(2^\circ\,\mathrm{N}\) and \(Q\) at \({@k1-2@}^\circ\,\mathrm{S}\).</p><p>(b) \({@D2@}\) nm is \({@k2@}^\circ\). \(R\) and \(S\) are on the same side, so their latitudes differ by \({@k2@}^\circ\): for example \(10^\circ\,\mathrm{N}\) and \({@k2+10@}^\circ\,\mathrm{N}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
